Microsoft services such as Outlook and Teams were disrupted for about 10 hours on Thursday as the tech giant struggled to fix issues with some servers, hitting businesses and government agencies globally that use the popular platforms. The company blamed elevated infrastructure loads and said it was working to restore services.
</description><media:thumbnail url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/70b604ac-deec-11f1-93fd-3f371022a770/w:1024/p:16x9/edca650364d04128c2ba1180b1d2b3e7b3ececc7.jpg"/><enclosure url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/70b604ac-deec-11f1-93fd-3f371022a770/w:1024/p:16x9/edca650364d04128c2ba1180b1d2b3e7b3ececc7.jpg" type="image/jpeg" length="0"/><guid isPermaLink="false">83f098e8-df14-11f1-8fc8-6703c19b14d1</guid><pubDate>Fri, 13 Nov 2026 03:44:22 GMT</pubDate><source url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/70b604ac-deec-11f1-93fd-3f371022a770/w:1024/p:16x9/edca650364d04128c2ba1180b1d2b3e7b3ececc7.jpg">© Saul Loeb, AFP</source><dc:creator>FRANCE 24</dc:creator></item><item><category>Americas</category><title>Pope Leo XIV meets abuse victims in Peru, says Church must make 'amends'</title><link>https://www.france24.com/en/americas/20261113-pope-leo-xiv-meets-abuse-victims-in-peru-says-church-must-make-amends</link><description>
Pope Leo XIV met six victims of clerical abuse in Peru on Thursday, including people linked to the disbanded Sodalitium Christianae Vitae. The US-born pontiff also urged the Catholic Church to make amends to survivors and strengthen protections against abuse as he returned to the country where he served for nearly two decades.
</description><media:thumbnail url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/74ed1b6a-ded2-11f1-a1e7-695cb59fade5/w:1024/p:16x9/e508fb7b23659b4d2a2f46189ca72db15c5d2dbc.jpg"/><enclosure url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/74ed1b6a-ded2-11f1-a1e7-695cb59fade5/w:1024/p:16x9/e508fb7b23659b4d2a2f46189ca72db15c5d2dbc.jpg" type="image/jpeg" length="0"/><guid isPermaLink="false">1d58660c-df0e-11f1-9817-c7a27ad55a29</guid><pubDate>Fri, 13 Nov 2026 02:58:33 GMT</pubDate><source url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/74ed1b6a-ded2-11f1-a1e7-695cb59fade5/w:1024/p:16x9/e508fb7b23659b4d2a2f46189ca72db15c5d2dbc.jpg">© Tiziana Fabi, AFP</source><dc:creator>FRANCE 24</dc:creator></item><item><category>Americas</category><title>Haitian police retake national stadium from gangs after two years</title><link>https://www.france24.com/en/americas/20261113-haitian-police-retake-national-stadium-from-gangs-after-two-years</link><description>
Haitian security forces regained control of the country's main football stadium on Thursday after two years under gang control, with Salvadoran troops taking over security at the venue. The operation comes as El Salvador deploys 2,000 soldiers to Haiti following an ambush that left four of its troops missing.
</description><media:thumbnail url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/3dd13174-df00-11f1-9b27-3f371022a770/w:1024/p:16x9/2026-11-12T233347Z-933808832-RC24MOADXTJ7-RTRMADP-3-HAITI-VIOLENCE-EL-SALVADOR.jpg"/><enclosure url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/3dd13174-df00-11f1-9b27-3f371022a770/w:1024/p:16x9/2026-11-12T233347Z-933808832-RC24MOADXTJ7-RTRMADP-3-HAITI-VIOLENCE-EL-SALVADOR.jpg" type="image/jpeg" length="0"/><guid isPermaLink="false">22c04a54-df06-11f1-97b0-1908cbabb3a5</guid><pubDate>Fri, 13 Nov 2026 02:01:26 GMT</pubDate><source url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/3dd13174-df00-11f1-9b27-3f371022a770/w:1024/p:16x9/2026-11-12T233347Z-933808832-RC24MOADXTJ7-RTRMADP-3-HAITI-VIOLENCE-EL-SALVADOR.jpg">© Fildor Pq Egeder, Reuters</source><dc:creator>FRANCE 24</dc:creator></item><item><category>Europe</category><title>Live: Zelensky and Kushner confirm that Europe will join next round of Russia-Ukraine talks</title><link>https://www.france24.com/en/europe/20261113-live-zelensky-and-kushner-confirm-that-europe-will-join-next-round-of-russia-ukraine-talks</link><description>
US envoy and President ​Donald Trump's son-in-law Jared Kushner said on Thursday Russia and ​Ukraine ‌agreed ⁠to expand the next ‌round of talks to include ⁠European nations, who will debate whether their envoy should be one individual or more and who should represent them at a summit in Dublin on Friday. Follow our liveblog for the latest updates.
</description><media:thumbnail url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/3f454e78-de83-11f1-a0a8-5f54d898a954/w:1024/p:16x9/2026-11-11T190604Z-1479843933-RC2VLOAJ8US1-RTRMADP-3-UKRAINE-CRISIS-IRELAND.jpg"/><enclosure url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/3f454e78-de83-11f1-a0a8-5f54d898a954/w:1024/p:16x9/2026-11-11T190604Z-1479843933-RC2VLOAJ8US1-RTRMADP-3-UKRAINE-CRISIS-IRELAND.jpg" type="image/jpeg" length="0"/><guid isPermaLink="false">7d7314f8-deee-11f1-9cec-ab45e2195d29</guid><pubDate>Fri, 13 Nov 2026 01:39:42 GMT</pubDate><source url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/3f454e78-de83-11f1-a0a8-5f54d898a954/w:1024/p:16x9/2026-11-11T190604Z-1479843933-RC2VLOAJ8US1-RTRMADP-3-UKRAINE-CRISIS-IRELAND.jpg">© Cathal McNaughton, Reuters</source><dc:creator>FRANCE 24</dc:creator></item><item><category>Spotlight</category><title>‘Peruvian pope’ Leo XIV comes home to a country that shaped his life and faith through 'hardship'</title><link>https://www.france24.com/en/tv-shows/spotlight/20261112-peruvian-pope-leo-xiv-comes-home-to-a-country-that-shaped-his-life-and-faith-through-hardship</link><description>
François Picard is pleased to welcome Victor Casallo, Director of the Department of Philosophical and Theological Studies at Antonio Ruiz de Montoya University. Can a Pope shaped by Peru's history of political violence, social inequality and religious activism help a deeply polarized nation heal and rediscover the possibility of dialogue? "We tend to see the other person not as someone who holds a different opinion, but as an enemy." Casallo argues that Pope Leo, the first American-born pontiff's identity cannot be understood through his Chicago origins alone.
</description><media:thumbnail url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/996a5eba-dee3-11f1-82e0-29048b396e03/w:1024/p:16x9/EN-20261112-181154-182641-CS.jpg"/><enclosure url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/996a5eba-dee3-11f1-82e0-29048b396e03/w:1024/p:16x9/EN-20261112-181154-182641-CS.jpg" type="image/jpeg" length="0"/><guid isPermaLink="false">6936791e-def1-11f1-96ef-bf46bbbd6e25</guid><pubDate>Thu, 12 Nov 2026 23:33:05 GMT</pubDate><dc:creator>François PICARD</dc:creator></item><item><category></category><title>‘Peruvian pope’ Leo XIV comes home to a country that shaped his life and faith through 'hardship'</title><link>https://www.france24.com/en/peruvian-pope-leo-xiv-comes-home-to-a-country-that-shaped-his-life-and-faith-through-hardship</link><description>
François Picard is pleased to welcome Victor Casallo, Director of the Department of Philosophical and Theological Studies at Antonio Ruiz de Montoya University. Can a Pope shaped by Peru's history of political violence, social inequality and religious activism help a deeply polarized nation heal and rediscover the possibility of dialogue? "We tend to see the other person not as someone who holds a different opinion, but as an enemy." Casallo argues that Pope Leo, the first American-born pontiff's identity cannot be understood through his Chicago origins alone. His decades of pastoral experience in Peru, amid political instability, terrorism, state violence and profound social inequalities, have shaped a religious outlook rooted in cultural openness, solidarity with the marginalized and an intimate understanding of human suffering.
</description><media:thumbnail url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/996a5eba-dee3-11f1-82e0-29048b396e03/w:1024/p:16x9/EN-20261112-181154-182641-CS.jpg"/><enclosure url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/996a5eba-dee3-11f1-82e0-29048b396e03/w:1024/p:16x9/EN-20261112-181154-182641-CS.jpg" type="image/jpeg" length="0"/><guid isPermaLink="false">9e9e7cb8-dee3-11f1-8c50-4bff3f955920</guid><pubDate>Thu, 12 Nov 2026 23:26:12 GMT</pubDate><dc:creator>FRANCE24</dc:creator></item><item><category>Americas</category><title>Eight killed, including two children, in New York building collapse</title><link>https://www.france24.com/en/americas/20261112-eight-killed-including-two-children-in-new-york-building-collapse</link><description>
Eight people, including two children, were killed when a residential building collapsed into an adjacent construction site in Brooklyn on Wednesday. Firefighters spent hours searching the rubble overnight before declaring the operation over on Thursday, as authorities launched an investigation into the disaster.
</description><media:thumbnail url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/82abc90a-deea-11f1-8c96-29048b396e03/w:1024/p:16x9/AP26316296072440.jpg"/><enclosure url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/82abc90a-deea-11f1-8c96-29048b396e03/w:1024/p:16x9/AP26316296072440.jpg" type="image/jpeg" length="0"/><guid isPermaLink="false">907174c0-deec-11f1-88b2-7b6870e6d6ea</guid><pubDate>Thu, 12 Nov 2026 22:58:23 GMT</pubDate><source url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/82abc90a-deea-11f1-8c96-29048b396e03/w:1024/p:16x9/AP26316296072440.jpg">© Ryan Murphy, AP</source><dc:creator>FRANCE 24</dc:creator></item><item><category>A propos</category><title>Russia using 'illegal and unethical means, knows Europe won't use same means back', expert says</title><link>https://www.france24.com/en/tv-shows/a-propos/20261112-russia-using-illegal-and-unethical-means-knows-europe-won-t-use-same-means-back-expert-says</link><description>
There are mounting concerns across Europe that recent hoax threats and cyber attacks could be part of a massive hybrid warfare campaign. FRANCE 24's Sharon Gaffney speaks with Elisabeth Braw, Senior Fellow with the Atlantic Council’s Transatlantic Security Initiative. She says that Russia's aim is to "increase its power at the expense of European countries and the easiest way to do that is to constantly wage this dirty war against European countries, using illegal and unethical means, because Russia knows that we are not going to use the same means back".
</description><media:thumbnail url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/630cef20-dee5-11f1-a08b-f71d22e7fea9/w:1024/p:16x9/EN-20261112-223114-224420-CS.jpg"/><enclosure url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/630cef20-dee5-11f1-a08b-f71d22e7fea9/w:1024/p:16x9/EN-20261112-223114-224420-CS.jpg" type="image/jpeg" length="0"/><guid isPermaLink="false">245c7212-dee7-11f1-ba78-d1df06cbd525</guid><pubDate>Thu, 12 Nov 2026 22:19:34 GMT</pubDate><dc:creator>Sharon GAFFNEY</dc:creator></item><item><category></category><title>Russia using 'illegal and unethical means, knows Europe won't use same means back', expert says</title><link>https://www.france24.com/en/russia-using-illegal-and-unethical-means-knows-europe-won-t-use-same-means-back-expert-says</link><description>
There are mounting concern across Europe that recent hoax threats and cyber attacks could be part of a massive hybrid warfare campaign. FRANCE 24's Sharon Gaffney speaks with Elisabeth Braw, Senior Fellow with the Atlantic Council’s Transatlantic Security Initiative. She says that Russia's aim is to "increase its power at the expense of European countries and the easiest way to do that is to constantly wage this dirty war against European countries, using illegal and unethical means, because Russia knows that we are not going to use the same means back".
</description><media:thumbnail url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/630cef20-dee5-11f1-a08b-f71d22e7fea9/w:1024/p:16x9/EN-20261112-223114-224420-CS.jpg"/><enclosure url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/630cef20-dee5-11f1-a08b-f71d22e7fea9/w:1024/p:16x9/EN-20261112-223114-224420-CS.jpg" type="image/jpeg" length="0"/><guid isPermaLink="false">68337cee-dee5-11f1-b754-1b093dc5fd5b</guid><pubDate>Thu, 12 Nov 2026 22:12:48 GMT</pubDate><dc:creator>FRANCE24</dc:creator></item><item><category>Business</category><title>Jensen Huang resigns as Nvidia CEO after mounting scandals</title><link>https://www.france24.com/en/tv-shows/business/20261112-jensen-huang-resigns-as-nvidia-ceo-after-mounting-scandals</link><description>
Jensen Huang, long considered the face of the AI revolution, has resigned as Nvidia's President, CEO and board member as sandals over his romantic affairs wiped hundreds of billions of dollars from the company's market value in a matter of days. US lawmakers signal they'll likely push for tighter export restrictions for AI chips. Meanwhile, the rapid buildout of AI data centre is sparking anger and worries among French communities. 
</description><media:thumbnail url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/531ee640-dee5-11f1-a2c0-5f54d898a954/w:1024/p:16x9/XX-NW-HDL-JENSEN-HUANG-RESIGNS-22H-AN-Meiqi-transfer.png"/><enclosure url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/531ee640-dee5-11f1-a2c0-5f54d898a954/w:1024/p:16x9/XX-NW-HDL-JENSEN-HUANG-RESIGNS-22H-AN-Meiqi-transfer.png" type="image/png" length="0"/><guid isPermaLink="false">0fc2498c-dee5-11f1-93c3-cd8d74486057</guid><pubDate>Thu, 12 Nov 2026 22:10:28 GMT</pubDate><dc:creator>Yuka ROYER</dc:creator></item><item><category>Truth or Fake</category><title>Mauritania fishing boats falsely portrayed as migrant fleet bound for Spain</title><link>https://www.france24.com/en/tv-shows/truth-or-fake/20261112-mauritania-fishing-boats-falsely-portrayed-as-migrant-fleet-bound-for-spain</link><description>
Spain’s Canary Islands are facing renewed pressure from migrant arrivals, with local authorities declaring a state of emergency. Against this backdrop, a viral video claims to show thousands of boats in Mauritania amassing at the port, that users said was "ready to set sail" to carry migrants to the archipelago. But the satellite images are misleading: they show a traditional fishing port, not a fleet of migrant vessels. Vedika Bahl explains in Truth or Fake.
</description><media:thumbnail url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/dc117dca-dee3-11f1-af98-5f54d898a954/w:1024/p:16x9/canaries.png"/><enclosure url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/dc117dca-dee3-11f1-af98-5f54d898a954/w:1024/p:16x9/canaries.png" type="image/png" length="0"/><guid isPermaLink="false">e8f45760-dee3-11f1-bbe6-07473b51b2e7</guid><pubDate>Thu, 12 Nov 2026 21:56:26 GMT</pubDate><dc:creator>Vedika BAHL</dc:creator></item><item><category>Across Africa</category><title>Across Africa: Tunisians drawn into Russia's war with Ukraine</title><link>https://www.france24.com/en/across-africa-tunisians-drawn-into-russia-s-war-with-ukraine</link><description>
This week: A new report says more than 1,400 Africans have been recruited to fight for Russia in Ukraine since 2023. Also, as South Africa races to become Africa's digital gateway, a growing backlash is emerging in Cape Town. And music, dance and theatre lit up Abéché as the city's arts festival in Chad drew crowds of young spectators.
</description><media:thumbnail url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/0fa95d74-deb7-11f1-91b4-3f371022a770/w:1024/p:16x9/MG00000333-VOD-00010414-01.jpg"/><enclosure url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/0fa95d74-deb7-11f1-91b4-3f371022a770/w:1024/p:16x9/MG00000333-VOD-00010414-01.jpg" type="image/jpeg" length="0"/><guid isPermaLink="false">4396d9f4-deb7-11f1-a47f-1fb8837136ad</guid><pubDate>Thu, 12 Nov 2026 21:26:17 GMT</pubDate><dc:creator>Clarisse FORTUNÉ</dc:creator></item><item><category>Eye on Africa</category><title>Ngo criticizes UN's response to massacre in South Sudan</title><link>https://www.france24.com/en/ngo-criticizes-un-s-response-to-massacre-in-south-sudan</link><description>
In tonight's edition, Amnesty International accuses both the government and UN peacekeepers of failing to protect civilians during a massacre in South Sudan that killed over 200 people. Also, more than a thousand people are prosecuted for illegal mining in Guinea. And a century after French colonial troops killed him, a Malagasy king has finally come home.
</description><media:thumbnail url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/cb037f5c-dedd-11f1-928e-29048b396e03/w:1024/p:16x9/DL00006225-VOD-00023665-01.jpg"/><enclosure url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/cb037f5c-dedd-11f1-928e-29048b396e03/w:1024/p:16x9/DL00006225-VOD-00023665-01.jpg" type="image/jpeg" length="0"/><guid isPermaLink="false">56d72ef2-dede-11f1-817c-9944036d14be</guid><pubDate>Thu, 12 Nov 2026 21:20:52 GMT</pubDate><dc:creator>Clarisse FORTUNÉ</dc:creator></item><item><category></category><title>'There's no such thing as safe' in contact sports, brain injury expert says</title><link>https://www.france24.com/en/there-s-no-such-thing-as-safe-in-contact-sports-brain-injury-expert-says</link><description>
Discussing the issue of contact sports and the threat to sports men and women of all ages, particularly in the light of the recent death, just last month, of a 17-year-old French woman after a head collision on the rugby field, FRANCE 24's Mark Owen speaks with Dr Daniel H Daneshvar, Associate Professor at Harvard Medical School. He says that "there's not such thing as safe" in contact sports, explaining that "if you get a lot of repeated hits to the head, then you're at risk for problems later in life, both in the near term and the long term".
</description><media:thumbnail url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/4ad153ba-dedb-11f1-b6ab-f71d22e7fea9/w:1024/p:16x9/EN-20261112-213615-214115-CS.jpg"/><enclosure url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/4ad153ba-dedb-11f1-b6ab-f71d22e7fea9/w:1024/p:16x9/EN-20261112-213615-214115-CS.jpg" type="image/jpeg" length="0"/><guid isPermaLink="false">502489cc-dedb-11f1-a300-f7a4097b808e</guid><pubDate>Thu, 12 Nov 2026 21:09:43 GMT</pubDate><dc:creator>FRANCE24</dc:creator></item><item><category></category><title>Israeli strike on Gaza hospital kills six, including surgeon, medics say</title><link>https://www.france24.com/en/israeli-strike-on-gaza-hospital-kills-six-including-surgeon-medics-say</link><description>
Israeli fire killed 11 people in Gaza on Thursday, six of them in the bombing of Nasser hospital in south Gaza early in the day, health officials said, the deadliest single strike since Israel's elections last month. FRANCE 24's Mark Owen speaks with Dr Goher Rahbour, a surgeon working at the Nasser Hospital.
</description><media:thumbnail url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/d8bb4c84-ded2-11f1-b9be-3f371022a770/w:1024/p:16x9/EN-20261112-203639-204648-CS.jpg"/><enclosure url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/d8bb4c84-ded2-11f1-b9be-3f371022a770/w:1024/p:16x9/EN-20261112-203639-204648-CS.jpg" type="image/jpeg" length="0"/><guid isPermaLink="false">ddeb127a-ded2-11f1-aba1-f38243617a56</guid><pubDate>Thu, 12 Nov 2026 20:03:55 GMT</pubDate><dc:creator>FRANCE24</dc:creator></item><item><category></category><title>Zelensky warns Europe over US-Russia talks on broader security arrangements</title><link>https://www.france24.com/en/zelensky-warns-europe-over-us-russia-talks-on-broader-security-arrangements</link><description>
Ukrainian President Volodymyr Zelensky warned European leaders Thursday to be wary of discussions between the United States and Russia about a new security arrangement for the continent. Before launching the war in 2022, the Kremlin delivered an ultimatum to NATO on Europe's security arrangements which included demands for the alliance to withdraw forces from eastern Europe and stop accepting new members. FRANCE 24's Caroline Baum reports.
</description><media:thumbnail url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/eb157cac-ded1-11f1-b7cf-29048b396e03/w:1024/p:16x9/EN-20261112-201030-201228-CS.jpg"/><enclosure url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/eb157cac-ded1-11f1-b7cf-29048b396e03/w:1024/p:16x9/EN-20261112-201030-201228-CS.jpg" type="image/jpeg" length="0"/><guid isPermaLink="false">f04e1dfa-ded1-11f1-bc1f-9dad418e8b43</guid><pubDate>Thu, 12 Nov 2026 19:53:15 GMT</pubDate><dc:creator>FRANCE24</dc:creator></item><item><category>The Debate</category><title>Plugged-in planet? COP31 host Turkey leads charge for electrification</title><link>https://www.france24.com/en/tv-shows/the-debate/20261112-plugged-in-planet-cop31-host-turkey-leads-charge-for-electrification</link><description>
It’s sweater weather in Paris. But even though November brings brisk temperatures and fall foliage, it doesn’t mean last summer’s record heat should fade from our memories. One of the suggested reasons why students are so boiling mad is the hell they endured back in June in overheated classrooms and baking shoebox apartments.
</description><media:thumbnail url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/b85cdb4e-decb-11f1-b36a-3f371022a770/w:1024/p:16x9/2026-11-12T165926Z-848197191-RC2GMOAAGTKD-RTRMADP-3-CLIMATE-COP31-1.jpg"/><enclosure url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/b85cdb4e-decb-11f1-b36a-3f371022a770/w:1024/p:16x9/2026-11-12T165926Z-848197191-RC2GMOAAGTKD-RTRMADP-3-CLIMATE-COP31-1.jpg" type="image/jpeg" length="0"/><guid isPermaLink="false">39f1a51e-decb-11f1-b224-a53d61a43d69</guid><pubDate>Thu, 12 Nov 2026 19:38:19 GMT</pubDate><dc:creator>François PICARD</dc:creator></item><item><category></category><title>White House reportedly discussing Howard Lutnick's future as Commerce secretary</title><link>https://www.france24.com/en/white-house-reportedly-discussing-howard-lutnick-s-future-as-commerce-secretary</link><description>
Senior White House officials are discussing whether ​Commerce Secretary Howard Lutnick should be pushed out in the wake of the spy scandal surrounding Nvidia's now ​former ‌CEO Jensen Huang, two people familiar ⁠with the situation told Reuters on Thursday. In June, Reuters reported that the Trump administration had held ⁠off from adding China’s AI startup DeepSeek, memory chipmaker CXMT and more than 100 other companies flagged as national security risks to a trade blacklist. FRANCE 24's Wassim Cornet reports from Los Angeles.
</description><media:thumbnail url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/d1601f00-decd-11f1-b247-695cb59fade5/w:1024/p:16x9/EN-20261112-200116-200925-CS.jpg"/><enclosure url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/d1601f00-decd-11f1-b247-695cb59fade5/w:1024/p:16x9/EN-20261112-200116-200925-CS.jpg" type="image/jpeg" length="0"/><guid isPermaLink="false">d6a11834-decd-11f1-abe9-77f1257bdda6</guid><pubDate>Thu, 12 Nov 2026 19:22:28 GMT</pubDate><dc:creator>FRANCE24</dc:creator></item><item><category></category><title>Winter is coming: Putin seeks to 'hammer Ukrainians, their power grid, their water supplies'</title><link>https://www.france24.com/en/video/20261112-winter-is-coming-putin-seeks-to-hammer-ukrainians-their-power-grid-their-water-supplies</link><description>
As renewed diplomatic efforts seek to bring an end to Russia's war on Ukraine, the prospect of a negotiated settlement remains unlikely due to Russia's irreconcilable territorial demands, competing visions of European security and an increasingly unpredictable and pro-Moscow American approach to mediation. For Russia expert Mark Galeotti, Executive Director of Mayak Intelligence and Honorary Professor at University College London's School of Slavonic and East European Studies, Moscow's maximalist vision conceals a more complex negotiating strategy. "The Russian approach is always to sound incredibly bullish and then make any concessions at the very last minute," he explains, suggesting that the Kremlin's uncompromising rhetoric should not necessarily be mistaken for an absolute refusal to negotiate. Yet the territorial dispute over Donbas presents a particularly formidable obstacle. Putin's determination to claim control over the entirety of the region confronts Ukraine's refusal to surrender territory it continues to hold. "That Donbas question is, for me, an intractable one," Galeotti observes, warning that neither side currently appears willing to cede territory necessary for a breakthrough. Whereas conventional diplomacy generally proceeds through discreet negotiations before culminating in political announcements, Trump's approach has effectively reversed that sequence. "Trump makes grand declarations and various people try desperately to make it happen."
</description><media:thumbnail url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/bd0cc890-dec4-11f1-b12e-3f371022a770/w:1024/p:16x9/EN-20261112-151200-152330-CS.jpg"/><enclosure url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/bd0cc890-dec4-11f1-b12e-3f371022a770/w:1024/p:16x9/EN-20261112-151200-152330-CS.jpg" type="image/jpeg" length="0"/><guid isPermaLink="false">c346f6b8-dec4-11f1-b79a-1b6dd5e61ef8</guid><pubDate>Thu, 12 Nov 2026 18:45:44 GMT</pubDate><dc:creator>FRANCE24</dc:creator></item><item><category>Middle East</category><title>Israel to ban far-left French politician Jean-Luc Mélenchon from entry</title><link>https://www.france24.com/en/middle-east/20261112-israel-to-ban-far-left-french-politician-jean-luc-m%C3%A9lenchon-from-entry</link><description>
The Israeli government said Thursday that it would ban far-left French leader Jean-Luc Mélenchon from entering the country after the French presidential contender said he would use French vessels to force aid into Gaza if he were elected. The announcement comes a day after France confirmed it had refused entry to the head of a body representing settlements in the occupied West Bank.
</description><media:thumbnail url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/33654616-dab0-11f1-ad25-695cb59fade5/w:1024/p:16x9/2026-10-14T100614Z-1152484815-RC2Y2OATJ5N2-RTRMADP-3-FRANCE-POLITICS.jpg"/><enclosure url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/33654616-dab0-11f1-ad25-695cb59fade5/w:1024/p:16x9/2026-10-14T100614Z-1152484815-RC2Y2OATJ5N2-RTRMADP-3-FRANCE-POLITICS.jpg" type="image/jpeg" length="0"/><guid isPermaLink="false">8866be1e-debc-11f1-bc46-a78d57209718</guid><pubDate>Thu, 12 Nov 2026 17:43:03 GMT</pubDate><source url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/33654616-dab0-11f1-ad25-695cb59fade5/w:1024/p:16x9/2026-10-14T100614Z-1152484815-RC2Y2OATJ5N2-RTRMADP-3-FRANCE-POLITICS.jpg">© Abdul Saboor, Reuters</source><dc:creator>FRANCE 24</dc:creator></item><item><category>France</category><title>‘Labelled terrorists’: How France is using anti-terror powers to deter protests</title><link>https://www.france24.com/en/france/20261112-labelled-terrorists-france-authorities-used-anti-terrorism-powers-to-deter-protests</link><description>
The French government has been accused of weaponising an anti-terror law adopted in the wake of the 2015 Paris attacks to keep dozens of people away from massive protests sweeping the country. Lawyers and rights groups denounce an abuse of power with potentially dangerous repercussions for individual liberties and the right to protest.
</description><media:thumbnail url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/fd886f68-d484-11f1-9138-695cb59fade5/w:1024/p:16x9/000-D4BK2ND.jpg"/><enclosure url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/fd886f68-d484-11f1-9138-695cb59fade5/w:1024/p:16x9/000-D4BK2ND.jpg" type="image/jpeg" length="0"/><guid isPermaLink="false">405ec3ce-deb1-11f1-be91-dda799059892</guid><pubDate>Thu, 12 Nov 2026 17:32:06 GMT</pubDate><source url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/fd886f68-d484-11f1-9138-695cb59fade5/w:1024/p:16x9/000-D4BK2ND.jpg">© Aris Messinis, AFP</source><dc:creator>Benjamin DODMAN</dc:creator></item><item><category></category><title><![CDATA[Concentration of power & wealth: Do tech CEOs have more authority, influence than elected officials?]]></title><link>https://www.france24.com/en/video/20261112-concentration-of-power-wealth-do-tech-ceos-have-more-authority-influence-than-elected-officials</link><description>
The resignation of Nvidia founder Jensen Huang has thrown one of the world's most iconic technology companies into a leadership crisis, whose ripple effects extend far beyond Silicon Valley. Nvidia occupies a unique position in a booming AI industruy marked by intensifying competition between the United States and China. Its leadership upheaval raises immediate questions about corporate succession. But it is also fueling a broader debate about economic resilience, national security and the political influence of big tech titans. For Gavin Bade, trade and economic policy reporter at The Wall Street Journal, the controversy illustrates the extraordinary concentration of economic power in a handful of corporations and the individuals who lead them. “Do these CEOs actually have more authority and more influence than our elected officials?”
</description><media:thumbnail url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/647eefce-deb4-11f1-b21c-5f54d898a954/w:1024/p:16x9/EN-20261112-160417-161419-CS.jpg"/><enclosure url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/647eefce-deb4-11f1-b21c-5f54d898a954/w:1024/p:16x9/EN-20261112-160417-161419-CS.jpg" type="image/jpeg" length="0"/><guid isPermaLink="false">69f5e66a-deb4-11f1-adfb-ad75dcdbac46</guid><pubDate>Thu, 12 Nov 2026 16:49:25 GMT</pubDate><dc:creator>FRANCE24</dc:creator></item><item><category></category><title>Russia's Vladimir Putin suggests no Ukraine compromise in meeting with US envoys</title><link>https://www.france24.com/en/video/20261112-russia-s-vladimir-putin-suggests-no-ukraine-compromise-in-meeting-with-us-envoys</link><description>
Russia's President Vladimir Putin indicated on Thursday, November 12, that the Kremlin would not compromise on its hardline demands to end the war in Ukraine, but offered a short-term ceasefire in a key frontline city during a meeting with US envoys. FRANCE 24's International Affairs Editor Philip Turle has more.
</description><media:thumbnail url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/1e9642f2-deb7-11f1-b9bd-f71d22e7fea9/w:1024/p:16x9/capture-38e7cc3a.jpg"/><enclosure url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/1e9642f2-deb7-11f1-b9bd-f71d22e7fea9/w:1024/p:16x9/capture-38e7cc3a.jpg" type="image/jpeg" length="0"/><guid isPermaLink="false">dca321bc-deb6-11f1-a549-63495a9c8bee</guid><pubDate>Thu, 12 Nov 2026 16:46:14 GMT</pubDate><dc:creator>FRANCE24</dc:creator></item><item><category>Middle East</category><title>Israel kills six in Gaza hospital strike as 9-month-old baby freezes to death</title><link>https://www.france24.com/en/middle-east/20261112-israel-kills-six-in-gaza-hospital-strike-as-9-month-old-baby-freezes-to-death</link><description>
An overnight Israeli air strike on Nasser Hospital in southern Gaza killed at least six Palestinians Thursday, including a teenage boy and a surgeon, medics said. Elsewhere in the devastated Palestinian territory, a nine-month-old baby died from the cold as families flee worsening flooding.
</description><media:thumbnail url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/2915f31c-dead-11f1-ba4a-695cb59fade5/w:1024/p:16x9/2026-11-12T043325Z-1772503551-RC23MOA2UKYO-RTRMADP-3-ISRAEL-PALESTINIANS-GAZA.jpg"/><enclosure url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/2915f31c-dead-11f1-ba4a-695cb59fade5/w:1024/p:16x9/2026-11-12T043325Z-1772503551-RC23MOA2UKYO-RTRMADP-3-ISRAEL-PALESTINIANS-GAZA.jpg" type="image/jpeg" length="0"/><guid isPermaLink="false">bb02b8d8-deac-11f1-8b1d-e74430e79b3b</guid><pubDate>Thu, 12 Nov 2026 16:18:24 GMT</pubDate><source url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/2915f31c-dead-11f1-ba4a-695cb59fade5/w:1024/p:16x9/2026-11-12T043325Z-1772503551-RC23MOA2UKYO-RTRMADP-3-ISRAEL-PALESTINIANS-GAZA.jpg">© Ramadan Abed, Reuters</source><dc:creator>FRANCE 24</dc:creator></item></channel></rss>
